WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:03.510
Press the rocker on your camera.

2
00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:07.782
Right there, light reached the film for about a three-hundredth of a second.

3
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.625
Three hundred of those fit into a single second.

4
00:00:11.375 --> 00:00:15.180
And the shutter that did it is one piece of printed plastic,

5
00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:17.415
with no bought spring anywhere in it.

6
00:00:17.165 --> 00:00:21.647
So how do you make plastic move that fast, the same way every time?

7
00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:26.887
Most people say: a strong spring, and a quick, careful finger.

8
00:00:26.637 --> 00:00:28.727
But fingers aren't alike.

9
00:00:28.477 --> 00:00:32.672
A slow press and a quick press would give you two different pictures.

10
00:00:32.422 --> 00:00:35.172
And this camera hasn't got a strong spring.

11
00:00:34.922 --> 00:00:36.983
The real answer is a click.

12
00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:42.690
To see why it matters, go back to 1839, when a picture took minutes.

13
00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:44.325
The shutter was a cap.

14
00:00:44.075 --> 00:00:47.005
You took it off, you counted, you put it back.

15
00:00:46.755 --> 00:00:51.910
In lesson one you did the same job yourself: open, count, close.

16
00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.759
Then plates got faster, and suddenly no hand was quick enough.

17
00:00:56.467 --> 00:01:02.297
In June 1878, Eadweard Muybridge set up twelve cameras beside a racetrack,

18
00:01:02.047 --> 00:01:04.857
with wires stretched across the track.

19
00:01:04.607 --> 00:01:11.069
As the horses ran past, they broke the wires, and each wire fired one camera's shutter.

20
00:01:10.819 --> 00:01:14.457
And the pictures showed something nobody had been able to see:

21
00:01:14.207 --> 00:01:17.677
a galloping horse with all four feet off the ground.

22
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:23.120
By the 1880s, a simple fast shutter was just a plate with a hole in it,

23
00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:24.940
dropped past the lens.

24
00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:28.913
Yours is the same idea, swung instead of dropped.

25
00:01:28.663 --> 00:01:32.013
The blade turns on a peg, and it has one window.

26
00:01:31.763 --> 00:01:35.223
At rest, solid plastic covers the lens.

27
00:01:34.973 --> 00:01:37.858
Press, and the blade swings across.

28
00:01:37.608 --> 00:01:40.653
For an instant the window is over the lens.

29
00:01:40.403 --> 00:01:42.601
Then it's solid plastic again.

30
00:01:43.333 --> 00:01:45.793
And the next press swings it back the other way.

31
00:01:45.543 --> 00:01:47.639
One press, one picture.

32
00:01:48.367 --> 00:01:50.342
So where does the speed come from?

33
00:01:50.092 --> 00:01:55.112
The blade's own rim is springy, and a tooth on that rim sits in a notch.

34
00:01:54.862 --> 00:01:56.707
When you press the rocker,

35
00:01:56.457 --> 00:02:00.420
the tooth pushes up the slope of the notch, and nothing moves yet.

36
00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:04.390
The force builds, to about 2.3 newtons by the model.

37
00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:08.845
And then the tooth jumps out, and all of that push is let go at once.

38
00:02:08.595 --> 00:02:11.260
The blade flies through its swing.

39
00:02:11.010 --> 00:02:13.777
It's exactly like snapping your fingers:

40
00:02:13.527 --> 00:02:17.950
the pressure builds until they slip, and the snap is always quick.

41
00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:23.482
Clockwork was doing this job by 1910, when the Compur shutter timed itself,

42
00:02:23.232 --> 00:02:26.490
up to a two-hundred-and-fiftieth of a second.

43
00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:30.647
Without the click, the blade would only ever go as fast as your finger.

44
00:02:31.367 --> 00:02:33.202
Let's put a number on that.

45
00:02:32.952 --> 00:02:38.147
Without the click, pushed slowly, the model gives about a twenty-eighth of a second.

46
00:02:37.897 --> 00:02:41.792
With the click, about a three-hundred-and-twenty-second.

47
00:02:41.542 --> 00:02:47.121
322 divided by 28 is about 11.5.

48
00:02:46.871 --> 00:02:53.816
In steps of light, 11.5 sits between 8 and 16, so it's about three and a half steps.

49
00:02:53.566 --> 00:02:57.945
Now, full sun on the small dot is already half a step over.

50
00:02:57.695 --> 00:03:00.515
Add three and a half and you're at four steps.

51
00:03:00.265 --> 00:03:02.115
Film forgives three.

52
00:03:01.865 --> 00:03:06.285
So a slow press would spoil the picture, and the click is what saves it.

53
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:12.575
No two presses are quite the same, of course: by the model, about half a step either way.

54
00:03:12.325 --> 00:03:14.450
And printers differ too.

55
00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:19.555
A print with thicker walls has a stronger spring in its rim, so it snaps faster.

56
00:03:19.305 --> 00:03:25.570
From the thinnest print to the thickest, the model's time runs from 1/176 to 1/423.

57
00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:28.060
That sounds like a lot,

58
00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:34.445
until you know that judging the sky by eye is only good to about one step.

59
00:03:34.195 --> 00:03:36.763
The shutter is steadier than you are.

60
00:03:37.467 --> 00:03:39.607
There's one more thing it can do.

61
00:03:39.357 --> 00:03:42.627
Press the rocker only halfway, to its middle click,

62
00:03:42.377 --> 00:03:45.627
and the window stops over the lens and stays open.

63
00:03:45.377 --> 00:03:47.792
Count, and then press on down.

64
00:03:47.542 --> 00:03:49.362
That's a time picture.

65
00:03:49.112 --> 00:03:53.627
By a window, small dot, ISO 400: count one.

66
00:03:53.377 --> 00:03:56.027
With the lamps on, count five.

67
00:03:55.777 --> 00:04:00.585
Stand the camera on something steady, because no hand holds still for a count.

68
00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:06.580
In your kit, all of this is one move: the blade drops onto a peg in the front plate.

69
00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:08.245
There's no spring to lose.

70
00:04:08.967 --> 00:04:12.997
So how does plastic move that fast, the same way every time?

71
00:04:12.747 --> 00:04:18.392
Your finger only loads the click, and the click lets go at the same force every time.

72
00:04:18.142 --> 00:04:22.053
Test it on yourself: try to snap your fingers slowly.

73
00:04:21.803 --> 00:04:24.498
However you press, the snap is quick.

74
00:04:24.248 --> 00:04:28.357
And try this: film the rocker in your phone's slow motion,

75
00:04:28.107 --> 00:04:30.808
and see if you can catch the window passing.

76
00:04:30.558 --> 00:04:33.282
So now you've got the hole and the time.

77
00:04:33.032 --> 00:04:35.957
But you only get one picture per piece of film,

78
00:04:35.707 --> 00:04:39.592
so the film has to move, by exactly the right amount.

79
00:04:39.342 --> 00:04:44.882
Next time: how does the camera know it has moved the film exactly one picture?

80
00:04:44.632 --> 00:04:48.277
Every picture here is drawn or simulated.

81
00:04:48.027 --> 00:04:51.160
No photograph has been taken with this camera yet.

